  "dom_id": "step-q48949",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/54c2987e0078899132d5a350a87e0e1283bd919e8fbe065b86574c2df2348e2f.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{\u2032}(3)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{\u2032}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(3)=\\frac{{f}^{\u2032}(3)\\cdot g(3)-f(3)\\cdot {g}^{\u2032}(3)}{(g(3))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(3)=-1$ and $g(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (2,5)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{\u2032}(3)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,\\frac{7}{2})$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{\u2032}(3)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(3) & =\\frac{{f}^{\u2032}(3)\\cdot g(3)-f(3)\\cdot {g}^{\u2032}(3)}{{(g(3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-1)\\cdot 1}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
